Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
1.2 Molecular cloud, integration aperture and laser-scatter background\
\>", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"2255055f-98fb-4965-a219-edb535c6a7ac"],

Cell["\<\
The cloud is a 2D Gaussian of measured FWHM 2.8 mm in object space at the MOT \
(sigma = FWHM/(2 Sqrt[2 Log[2]]) = 1.189 mm, 1/e^2 diameter 4.76 mm). The \
FWHM is the primary input; sigma and the 1/e^2 radius derive from it. The \
integration aperture is a disc of radius 2 sigma = 2.378 mm at the MOT, which \
contains a fraction 1 - Exp[-2] = 86.5% of the cloud fluorescence. Signal \
photon numbers quoted in this notebook (nSig) are the photons arriving on the \
sensor inside that aperture. All cloud geometry is kept in object space and \
pixels are projected back through the magnification: a superpixel of physical \
pitch b x pixelPitch on the chip covers b x pixelPitch / M at the MOT. \
Background and dark electrons, in contrast, are properties of the physical \
sensor pixel and do not scale with M.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"30d5291c-fb69-4f78-b82e-29a737955ad5"],

Cell["\<\
The laser-scatter background is specified as 39 photons/s per 6.5 um x 6.5 um \
pixel at baseline; the active condition is 4x baseline, i.e. 3.69 photons/(s \
um^2). The multiplier is a free parameter (0.5 to 10).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"db75a368-10eb-467a-97ce-3b4da953efcf"],

Cell["\<\
Molecule number to signal photons: each molecule scatters scatteringRate \
photons/s, of which the fraction geometricEfficiency x transmissionOptics \
reaches the sensor and apertureSignalFraction of those land inside the \
integration aperture, so nSig = nMol x scatteringRate x tExp x \
geometricEfficiency x transmissionOptics x apertureSignalFraction. The \
scattering rate (default 1e6 photons/s per molecule, a typical CaF MOT value) \
is an assumed parameter and is exposed as a dashboard control; all benchmark \
axes are in molecules.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6623fb2c-abfa-409c-939f-d0428d9adac7"],

